  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 220 AM MDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nBit of a quiet weekend ahead as the upper level ridge restrengthens \nover the Four Corners region, which will bring hot temperatures and \ndrier conditions to the region. Main concern will be the heat, \nparticularly Sunday and Monday where we currently have a Heat \nAdvisory in effect for the I-25 Corridor and portions of the\nnorthern plains. \n\nTemperatures will reach up to the mid-90s for much of the plains \ntoday, with overall clear skies throughout the day. Due to the \nnorth/northwest flow aloft, smoke from wildfires in the Pacific \nnorthwest will likely filter in again. This may create hazy skies. \nFor the mountains, only a few weak showers possible (~20% chance) in \nthe afternoon. \n\nBy Sunday, the axis of the mid-level thermal ridge will shift east \ninto Colorado. 700-mb temperatures will reach up to 19-22 C, which \ntranslates to upper 90s to low 10s across the plains. We could break \ndaily temperature records Sunday and/or Monday. Denver's current \nrecord for August 2 is 101 F set in 2012, and the current forecast \nis 100 F. A Heat Advisory remains in effect for the I-25 Corridor \nand portions of the northern plains, including Greeley, Sunday \nthrough Monday. \n\nIt still looks like we will see a brief reprieve of the heat after \nMonday, as an upper level trough treks across the northern U.S. This \nwill bring a cold front down sometime Monday night/Tuesday \nmorning. Ensemble guidance continues to indicate scattered showers\nand thunderstorms behind the front on Tuesday. However, there \ndoesn't seem to be enough moisture to produce widespread \nprecipitation/beneficial rainfall at this time.\n\nThere is still some uncertainty with how long the \"cooler\" air \nsticks around, with ensemble guidance varying between models and \nmodel runs. NBM has trended slightly cooler with this forecast \npackage and will probably continue to do so as we get closer to next \nweek. The plains will likely be somewhere in the 80s for Wednesday. \n\nTowards the end of the week, global models favor hot temperatures \nreturning, with forecasted highs in the upper 90s again. Due to the \nupper level ridge in the desert southwest and resulting northwest \nflow aloft, doesn't look like we will receive much monsoon moisture. \nAside from isolated afternoon showers and weak thunderstorms, dry\nconditions should prevail.",
